That’s where the library stops being a collection of metaphors and becomes a mechanism synthesizer.

The key is not to ask, “What machine does a gear + spring + Seal resemble?” Instead:

What behavior do I want, and which primitives compose into a system that produces it?

Treat every mechanical, biological, computational, symbolic, and control primitive as a typed component.

p_i=(I_i,O_i,F_i,S_i,C_i)

where I/O are accepted inputs/outputs, F is its transformation, S its internal state, and C its constraints.

Then machine synthesis becomes:

\boxed{
M^\*=\arg\max_M
[\text{desired behavior}(M)-\text{cost}-\text{instability}-\text{failure}]
}

subject to compatible interfaces.

The universal construction grammar

You can reduce a surprising number of functional systems to a small set of roles:

SOURCE
  ↓
SENSE ───────────────┐
  ↓                  │
FILTER                │
  ↓                  │
DECIDE                │
  ↓                  │
TRANSFORM             │
  ↓                  │
TRANSMIT              │
  ↓                  │
ACTUATE                │
  ↓                  │
ENVIRONMENT            │
  ↓                  │
RESULT ──→ MEASURE ───┘
              ↓
            MEMORY
              ↓
            LEARN

Now populate those slots from completely different domains.

A bearing can become decoupling. A gear becomes ratio transformation. A clutch becomes conditional coupling. A spring becomes energy/information storage. A governor becomes negative feedback. A neuron contributes threshold activation. Immune systems contribute self/non-self discrimination and adaptive memory. Evolution contributes variation + selection + inheritance. Ant colonies contribute local rules + distributed coordination. Plants contribute resource-seeking growth under environmental constraints.

The physical implementations remain radically different. The functional relations can sometimes be shared.

Example: invent a self-stabilizing explorer

Give the synthesizer the specification:

DESIRE = {
    "explore_unknown_space": True,
    "maintain_core_invariants": True,
    "recover_from_disturbance": True,
    "learn_from_failed_routes": True,
    "avoid_runaway": True,
}

It searches the primitive library and constructs:

Garden       → generate possibilities
Prism        → explore several representations
Differential → allow branches to diverge
Bearings     → prevent branches interfering prematurely
Gyroscope    → maintain invariant orientation
Governor     → bound exploration
Reality      → sample external evidence
Wake         → preserve trajectory
Decrypto     → extract information from failures
Metamap      → update future routing

That’s already a functional architecture.

Call it Semantic Starfish:

                [Explorer]
                    │
        ┌───────────┼───────────┐
        ↓           ↓           ↓
      Arm A       Arm B       Arm C
        │           │           │
      Portal      Mirror       Garden
        │           │           │
        └─────→ DIFFERENTIAL ←──┘
                    │
                REALITY
                    │
               GYROSCOPE
                    │
                 CORE
                 ╱   ╲
             WAKE    METAMAP

Lose one exploratory arm? Core operation survives. Find something useful on another? Feed its afterglow inward.

That’s borrowing the organizational logic of biological redundancy/regeneration without claiming we’ve reproduced actual starfish biology.

Now let machines breed

This gets much more interesting when construction isn’t manual.

Represent a candidate machine as a graph:

M=(V,E)

where nodes are primitives and edges are permitted interfaces.

Then:

def invent_machine(goal, primitive_library):
    population = random_assemblies(primitive_library)
    while True:
        for machine in population:
            behavior = simulate(machine)
            machine.fitness = score(
                behavior,
                goal,
                # reward
                function=True,
                robustness=True,
                efficiency=True,
                adaptability=True,
                # punish
                instability=True,
                complexity=True,
                failure=True,
            )
        survivors = select_best(population)
        offspring = []
        for machine in survivors:
            offspring += mutate(machine)
            offspring += recombine(machine, survivors)
        population = survivors + offspring
        if solved(population, goal):
            return best(population)

Now we’ve imported another primitive:

\boxed{\text{Evolution}=
\text{variation}+\text{selection}+\text{inheritance}}

The synthesizer doesn’t have to know beforehand what the machine should look like.

It needs a fitness function.

That connects directly to your aiming work:

\text{Aim}
\rightarrow
\text{desired consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{fitness function}
\rightarrow
\text{machine search}

And Metamap changes the search

Naive evolutionary search generates enormous amounts of garbage.

Metamap says:

“Machines with this terrain and objective historically benefit from governor + differential + feedback before adding recursion.”

So instead of random assembly:

candidate = random_machine()

you get:

candidate = metamap.seed_machine(
    desired_behavior,
    terrain,
    constraints
)

Then the Afterglow records why assemblies failed.

Perhaps:

Spring + positive feedback
→ oscillation
Governor + spring + damper
→ stable oscillation
Differential before governor
→ branches destabilize
Governor before differential
→ stable independent branches

Those become machine fossils.

And the Indigo Hammer hits those too:

1000\text{ successful machines}
\rightarrow
\text{shared topology}
\rightarrow
\text{new primitive}

A complicated assembly can eventually be compressed into a new Seal.

So you get a hierarchy:

\text{primitive}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{machine}
\rightarrow
\text{organism}
\rightarrow
\text{ecology}

But then Hammer recognizes that the hierarchy itself isn’t sacred.

A machine can contain an ecology.
An ecology can function as a machine.
An organism can become a component.
A Seal can represent an entire previously evolved machine.

So the better abstraction is nested functional graphs.

The really powerful synthesis loop

DESIRED CONSEQUENCE
        ↓
     METAMAP
        ↓
retrieve useful mechanisms
        ↓
   COMMAND TABLE
        ↓
assemble candidate machines
        ↓
     SIMULATE
     ↙      ↘
 FAILURE   SUCCESS
    ↓         ↓
DECRYPTO    REALITY
    ↓         ↓
AFTERGLOW ← RESULT
        ↓
    MACHINE FOSSILS
        ↓
 INDIGO HAMMER
        ↓
discover shared mechanism
        ↓
     NEW SEAL
        ↓
add to primitive library
        ↓
   BUILD AGAIN
        ↺

And that’s where the recursive compounding becomes genuine in software.

Generation 1 searches with:

P_0

Generation 2 searches with:

P_1=P_0+\text{new discovered primitives}

Generation n:

P_{n+1}=P_n+\Delta P_n

So you’re not merely searching for better machines.

You’re searching for better machines while simultaneously discovering better building blocks for searching the next design space.

That’s a credible computational interpretation of the biological analogy: evolution of designs plus evolution of the representation used to design them.

And I’d put one hard constraint around the whole architecture:

A metaphor earns promotion to mechanism only when its translated behavior can be specified, simulated, measured, and tested.

That keeps the Pantheon capable of traveling through extremely strange semantic spaces without confusing an evocative analogy for an engineered machine.

Imagine freely → Hammer → specify → compose → simulate → test → retain → Seal → recurse.